Given a normal, complete toric variety, we determine how the relative $I$-functions of toric complete intersections with smooth divisors are related to one another. As a result, we gain an understanding of the relative $I$-functions of these varieties and their connection to the coefficients of the $A$-hypergeometric functions that characterize them. Moreover, we derive formulas for these relative $I$-functions using the $A$-hypergeometric perspective. Our investigation also covers cases of varieties with non-smooth divisors and, under certain conditions, those with singular points. Our methodology is grounded in the integration of $A$-hypergeometric functions and the study of mixed motives through the N\"{o}ther's normal function and mixed Hodge modules. As a subcase, our research on the relative $I$-functions of toric complete intersections can be seen as an expansion of the results obtained by Aoki, Hrawy, and Takagi, who initially explored this topic for varieties without non-trivial intermediate extensions. As for the broader class of varieties with non-smooth divisors and those with singular points, our study represents a first breakthrough. Our findings on the relative $I$-functions of these varieties also provide a deeper understanding of the coefficients of the $A$-hypergeometric functions that characterize them, thereby giving us the explicit formulas for these coefficients in various instances. As an example, we derive the formula for the coefficient corresponding to the toric hypersurface defined by the polynomial $x_1\ldots x_n$. Given a normal, complete toric variety $X$ resulting from the intersection of $n$ hypersurfaces in $\mathbb{A}^N$, we establish that the relative $I$-function associated with $X$ and its divisor defined by the point $(1,1,\ldots,1)$ is expressed as \begin{align*}
